stype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/23","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7835529","title":"SCC25 cells, ACGs 1"},{"accession":"GSM7835532","title":"SCC25 cells, DMSO 1"},{"accession":"GSM7835523","title":"SCC15 cells, ACGs 1"},{"accession":"GSM7835526","title":"SCC15 cells, DMSO 1"},{"accession":"GSM7835530","title":"SCC25 cells, ACGs 2"},{"accession":"GSM7835524","title":"SCC15 cells, ACGs 2"},{"accession":"GSM7835527","title":"SCC15 cells, DMSO 2"},{"accession":"GSM7835533","title":"SCC25 cells, DMSO 2"},{"accession":"GSM7835534","title":"SCC25 cells, DMSO 3"},{"accession":"GSM7835528","title":"SCC15 cells, DMSO 3"},{"accession":"GSM7835531","title":"SCC25 cells, ACGs 3"},{"accession":"GSM7835525","title":"SCC15 cells, ACGs 3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39823459"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE245nnn/GSE245065/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1026877"},"200295234":{"uid":"200295234","accession":"GSE295234","gds":"","title":"scRNA seq of in vitro co- culture of distinct subtypes of oral cancer associated primary fibroblasts and oral cancer cell line","summary":"Heterogeneity in oral cancer associated fibroblasts (CAFs)in tumor microenvironment modulate plasticity of malignant epithelial cells to drive tumorigenesis, ultimately leading to poorer clinical outcome. We identified a receptor tyrosin kinase 'TEK' or Tie2 involved in myofibroblastic differentiation of CAFs. Differentiated CAFs induce stochastic plasticity in oral cancer cells both in stemness and EMT axes. Inhibiting Tie2 in myofibroblastically differentiated CAFs purturbed their cancer cell remodelling ability. Here in this study we performed scRNA seq analysis to understand distinct CAF driven transcriptionl states of cancer cells to undertsand CAF driven cancer cells reprogramming in a higher resolution.","gpl":"24676","gse":"295234","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/21","suppfile":"MTX, TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8944065","title":"cancer cells with C2 CAF"},{"accession":"GSM8944066","title":"cancer cells with Tie2 inhibited C2 CAF"},{"accession":"GSM8944064","title":"cancer cells with C1 CAF"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":3,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40349056"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE295nnn/GSE295234/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1253738"},"200224803":{"uid":"200224803","accession":"GSE224803","gds":"","title":"A new model: radiation and cigarette smoke promote an aggressive cell phenotype and gene signature in head and neck cancer cells.  and gene signature in head and neck cancer cells.","summary":"Background and purpose: Mucosal Head and Neck squamous cell cancers (HNSCCs) are aggressive invasive cancers. They arise in the oral cavity, oropharynx, nasopharynx, hypopharynx and larynx. To control local disease, radiation is used curatively and palliatively to destroy or reduce the tumour load. Smokers are at increased risk of developing these cancers, and smoking whilst receiving radiation worsens survival rates. However, the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying this has not been well described. We developed a model to elucidate the effects of radiation and smoking and the pathways involved. This will enable the identification of novel pharmaceutical targets for patient trials. Materials & Methods: FaDu hypopharyngeal cancer cells were subjected to a fractionated daily radiation procedure and cultured in smoke exposed media. Cells were examined for changes in tumoursphere number, proliferation, migration, invasion, CD44 and ALDH1 expression, and next generation sequencing was performed. Immunohistochemistry compared CD44 and ALDH1 expression in never-smokers and smokers. Results: Radiation and smoking separately reduced FaDu Cancer Stem Cell (CSC) number and proliferation, and increased cell migration and invasion. Combined they further reduced CSC number and proliferation, and promoted migration. Through sequencing radiation and smoking produced a unique gene signature related to cell invasion, angiogenesis, and survival. CD44 expression and ALDH activity decreased after co-treatment and their expression did not associate with smoking status in a small patient cohort.","gpl":"24676","gse":"224803","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/12","suppfile":"CSV, RESULTS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7032578","title":"FaDu_control_3"},{"accession":"GSM7032587","title":"FaDu_smoke_radiation_3"},{"accession":"GSM7032581","title":"FaDu_smoke_3"},{"accession":"GSM7032584","title":"FaDu_radiation_3"},{"accession":"GSM7032577","title":"FaDu_control_2"},{"accession":"GSM7032580","title":"FaDu_smoke_2"},{"accession":"GSM7032583","title":"FaDu_radiation_2"},{"accession":"GSM7032586","title":"FaDu_smoke_radiation_2"},{"accession":"GSM7032585","title":"FaDu_smoke_radiation_1"},{"accession":"GSM7032582","title":"FaDu_radiation_1"},{"accession":"GSM7032576","title":"FaDu_control_1"},{"accession":"GSM7032579","title":"FaDu_smoke_1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40282522"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE224nnn/GSE224803/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA932731"},"200294386":{"uid":"200294386","accession":"GSE294386","gds":"","title":"Bulk RNA seq of in vitro co- culture of distinct subtypes of oral cancer associated primary fibroblasts and oral cancer cell line","summary":"Heterogeneity in oral cancer associated fibroblasts (CAFs)in tumor microenvironment modulate plasticity of malignant epithelial cells to drive tumorigenesis, ultimately leading to poorer clinical outcome. Myofibroblastically differentiated CAFs induce stochastic plasticity in oral cancer cells. We co-cultured non-myofibroblasts and myofibroblasts with oral cancer cell lines to understand distinct CAF driven cancer cell transcriptional reprogramming.","gpl":"16791","gse":"294386","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/11","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8903696","title":"SCC070 cells co-cultured with C2 CAF biol rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM8903699","title":"C1 CAF co-cultured with SCC070 cells biol rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM8903694","title":"SCC070 cells co-cultured with C1 CAF biol rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM8903697","title":"SCC070 cells co-cultured with C2 CAF biol rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM8903700","title":"C2 CAF co-cultured with SCC070 cells biol rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM8903695","title":"SCC070 cells co-cultured with C1 CAF biol rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM8903698","title":"C1 CAF co-cultured with SCC070 cells biol rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM8903701","title":"C2 CAF co-cultured with SCC070 cells biol rep 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40349056"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE294nnn/GSE294386/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1249372"},"200293370":{"uid":"200293370","accession":"GSE293370","gds":"","title":"Morphology-guided organoid classification reveals prognosis of oral cancer [dataset 1]","summary":"Oral cancer is an aggressive malignancy with a survival rate below 50% in advanced stages due to low mutation rates, lack of molecular subtypes, and limited treatment targets. This study presents a pioneering approach to classifying oral cancer subtypes based on the morphology of patient-derived organoids (PDOs) and proposes a novel therapeutic strategy. We successfully established 76 cancer and 81 normal PDOs. For cancer PDOs, both manual classification and AI-based scoring were utilized to categorize them into three distinct subtypes: normal-like, dense, and grape-like. These subtypes correlated with unique transcriptomic profiles, genetic mutations, and clinical outcomes, with patients harboring dense and grape-like organoids exhibiting poorer prognoses. Furthermore, drug response assessments of 14 single agents and Cisplatin combination therapies identified a synergistic treatment approach for resistant subtypes. This study highlights the potential of integrating morphology-based classification with genomic and transcriptomic analyses to refine oral cancer subtyping and develop effective treatment strategies.","gpl":"24676","gse":"293370","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/02","suppfile":"CSV, XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8881040","title":"SOC44T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881020","title":"OC60T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881037","title":"SOC26T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881017","title":"OC53T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881043","title":"SOC63T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881023","title":"OC80T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881029","title":"OC144T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881049","title":"SOC99T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881026","title":"OC122T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881046","title":"SOC70T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881015","title":"OC40T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881032","title":"SOC6T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881012","title":"OC25T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881035","title":"SOC22T-O RNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8881024","title":"OC