ls, highlighting a metabolic vulnerability in HNSCC and demonstrating a direct link between cholesterol metabolism and ER stress in cancer cell viability.","gpl":"24676","gse":"285603","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/04/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8705534","title":"C51, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705537","title":"N03, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705526","title":"C09, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705543","title":"N21, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705523","title":"C06, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705540","title":"N07, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705546","title":"N30, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705522","title":"C04, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705539","title":"N06, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705542","title":"N09, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705525","title":"C08, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705531","title":"C31, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705545","title":"N26, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705528","title":"C22, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705544","title":"N22, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705551","title":"N57, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705524","title":"C07, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705547","title":"N31, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705548","title":"N38, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705527","title":"C21, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705533","title":"C43, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705550","title":"N51, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705530","title":"C30, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705536","title":"C59, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705552","title":"N59, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705549","title":"N43, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705541","title":"N08, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705521","title":"C03, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705538","title":"N04, non-tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705532","title":"C38, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705529","title":"C26, tumor"},{"accession":"GSM8705535","title":"C57, tumor"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":32,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40456399"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE285nnn/GSE285603/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1204948"},"200273463":{"uid":"200273463","accession":"GSE273463","gds":"","title":"Mapping EGFR-mediated local invasion and response to Cetuximab in head and neck cancer","summary":"Recurrent/metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M-HNSCC) is a severe, frequently lethal condition. Targeted therapy includes epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) blockade, but overall response rates are low and predictive biomarkers amiss. Deepened understanding of molecular networks governing EGFR-driven progression are required to explore predictive markers and therapeutic targets. We have mapped EGFR-mediated local invasion in a 3D-model using photoconvertible cell-tracers and uncovered invasion-associated, differentially expressed genes (DEGs) correlated with EGFR/MAPK activity and epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) in primary malignant cells. A gene regulatory network (GRN) of local invasion was defined, composed of the central hubs inhibin subunit beta alpha (INHBA) and SLUG, and druggable effectors integrin subunit beta 4 (ITGβ4), its ligand laminin 5 (LAMB3/LAMC2), sphingosine kinase 1 (SPHK1), and EGFR ligand amphiregulin (AREG). Blockade of INHBA repressed local invasion, which was rescued by Activin A, Laminin 5, and sphingosine-1-phosphate, proving functional interconnection of the GRN. Functional DEGs (fDEGs) and, most prominently, central hub IHNBA, were associated with tumor buds in TCGA-HNSCC. Newly defined EGFR-activity subtypes, primarily dictated by AREG and epiregulin (EREG), show enhanced fDEGs expression, EMT correlation, and prognostic value. Cell-communication revealed strong interactions of AREG/EREG-governed EGFR-activities with non-malignant cells, potentially impacting on tumor progression. High expression of fDEGs enriched in EGFR-activities (INHBA, ITGA3, ITGΒ4, LAMA3, LAMC2, MT2A, EREG) was associated with response to Cetuximab in PDX models and R/M-HNSCC patients. Thus, we describe a novel GRN of EGFR-mediated local invasion and define druggable effectors with predictive potential regarding the response to Cetuximab.","gpl":"24676","gse":"273463","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/03/26","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8428837","title":"FaDu cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted invasive cells_Sample 4"},{"accession":"GSM8428854","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted invasive cells_Sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM8428834","title":"FaDu cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted invasive cells_Sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM8428860","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids under serum-free condition_Sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM8428857","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted invasive cells_Sample 4"},{"accession":"GSM8428828","title":"FaDu cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted non-invasive cells_Sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM8428825","title":"FaDu cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF in combination with Cetuximab_Sample 4"},{"accession":"GSM8428851","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF in combination with MEK-inhibitor_Sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM8428831","title":"FaDu cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF in combination with MEK-inhibitor_Sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM8428848","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted non-invasive cells_Sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM8428855","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated with high-dose EGF_Photoconverted invasive cells_Sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM8428852","title":"Kyse30 cell spheroids treated wit