ways, which are essential for tumor development. The proteogenomic analysis highlighted pathways critical for lymph node dissemination and factors contributing to an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment. Elevated levels of POSTN were found to reorganize the extracellular matrix (ECM), interact with TGF-β, disrupt cell cycle regulation, and suppress the immune response by reducing VCAM1 activity. Integrated analyses of single-cell and spatial transcriptome data revealed that cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) secrete TGF-β1/2, promoting cancer cell metastasis through epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT). Our integrated multi-omics analysis provides a detailed understanding of molecular mechanisms driving lymph node metastasis of OSCC. These insights could lead to more precise diagnostics and targeted treatments.","gpl":"24676","gse":"289930","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/03/12","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8801777","title":"QM11-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801771","title":"QM46-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801754","title":"QM33-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801774","title":"QM62-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801760","title":"QM43-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801780","title":"QM19-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801763","title":"QM18-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801783","title":"QM25-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801757","title":"QM26-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801759","title":"QM41-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801779","title":"QM14-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801782","title":"QM21-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801756","title":"QM08-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801776","title":"QM15-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801762","title":"QM06-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801765","title":"QM39-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801768","title":"QM17-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801764","title":"QM24-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801770","title":"QM40-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801753","title":"QM10-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801773","title":"QM61-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801761","title":"QM50-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801781","title":"QM20-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801767","title":"QM12-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801769","title":"QM30-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801784","title":"QM59-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801766","title":"QM56-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801778","title":"QM13-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801772","title":"QM48-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801775","title":"QM63-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801752","title":"QM09-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801755","title":"QM42-Tumor-RNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8801758","title":"QM27-Tumor-RNAseq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":33,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40038875"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE289nnn/GSE289930/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1225597"},"200289886":{"uid":"200289886","accession":"GSE289886","gds":"","title":"An Intraepithelial ILC1-like Natural Killer Cell Subset Produces IL-13","summary":"Natural killer (NK) cells are innate immune effectors with considerable heterogeneity and potent antitumor capabilities. Intraepithelial ILC1 (ieILC1)-like NK cells, a population of cytotoxic tissue-resident innate lymphoid cells, have recently been documented in the microenvironment of head and neck squamous cell carcinomas (HNSCC) and other solid tumors. These cells have antitumor cytolytic potential and are potent producers of type 1 cytokines, including IFNy. Here, we identify a subpopulation of ex vivo differentiated ieILC1-like NK cells that produce IL-13 upon stimulation. Functional characterization revealed that these cells co-expressed IFNy and IL-13 while maintaining an ILC1 transcriptional signature. IL-13 was induced either upon co-culture with tumor cell lines, or in response to TGF-B and IL-15. IL-13-expressing ieILC1-like NK cells were identified among tumor infiltrating lymphocytes expanded from patient HNSCC tumors, in support of their existence in primary tumors. These data demonstrate additional heterogeneity within the ieILC1-like NK cell population than previously appreciated and highlight a unique form of ILC plasticity in which cells with clear ILC1 transcriptional profiles express a type 2 cytokine. With the known roles of IL-13 in cancer cell growth dynamics and immunoregulation, the identification of this subset within tumor microenvironments presents a potential target for therapeutic manipulation.","gpl":"24676","gse":"289886","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/03/06","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8800919","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13+, Donor 32"},{"accession":"GSM8800922","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13-, Donor 35"},{"accession":"GSM8800921","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13+, Donor 35"},{"accession":"GSM8800924","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13-, Donor 43"},{"accession":"GSM8800920","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13-, Donor 32"},{"accession":"GSM8800923","title":"PMA/Iono ieILC1-like NK cells, IL-13+, Donor 43"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40114916"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE289nnn/GSE289886/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1225247"},"200260728":{"uid":"200260728","accession":"GSE260728","gds":"","title":"Transcriptome alteration of cancer stem cells (CSCs) and non-CSCs when CSCs were depleted in HNSCC","summary":"To investigate the  difference of s