12","title":"HN120Pri, H3K4me3, Rep1, Cell66"},{"accession":"GSM6518514","title":"HN120PCR, H3K27ac, Rep1, Cell1"},{"accession":"GSM6517804","title":"HN137PCR, H3K4me3, Rep2, Cell27"},{"accession":"GSM6519613","title":"HN137PCR, H3K27ac, Rep1, Cell184"},{"accession":"GSM6517223","title":"HN137Pri, H3K4me3, Rep4, Cell90"},{"accession":"GSM6517675","title":"HN137Met, H3K4me3, Rep2, Cell178"},{"accession":"GSM6517306","title":"HN137Pri, H3K4me3, Rep4, Cell177"},{"accession":"GSM6517389","title":"HN137Met, H3K4me3, Rep1, Cell72"},{"accession":"GSM6517592","title":"HN137Met, H3K4me3, Rep2, Cell91"},{"accession":"GSM6518903","title":"HN137Pri, H3K27ac, Rep1, Cell22"},{"accession":"GSM6518986","title":"HN137Pri, H3K27ac, Rep1, Cell109"},{"accession":"GSM6518700","title":"HN120PCR, H3K27ac, Rep2, Cell3"},{"accession":"GSM6518617","title":"HN120PCR, H3K27ac, Rep1, Cell108"},{"accession":"GSM6516725","title":"HN120PCR, H3K4me3, Rep2, Cell147"},{"accession":"GSM6517011","title":"HN137Pri, H3K4me3, Rep2, Cell157"},{"accession":"GSM6516513","title":"HN120PCR, H3K4me3, Rep1, Cell119"},{"accession":"GSM6519115","title":"HN137Pri, H3K27ac, Rep2, Cell50"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":3973,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39622638"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE212nnn/GSE212250/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA874747"},"200212247":{"uid":"200212247","accession":"GSE212247","gds":"","title":"Deciphering the function of intrinsic and genomics-driven epigenetic heterogeneity in head and neck cancer progression with single-nucleus CUT&RUN [bulk RNAseq]","summary":"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has proven to be an invaluable tool for the study of tumor evolution. However, interrogating regulatory epigenetic alterations during cancer progression at the resolution of single-cells has remained a major challenge due to the lack of sensitivity of current assays. Here, we developed the highly-sensitive single-nucleus CUT&RUN (snCUT&RUN) assay to profile histone modifications in primary, metastatic, and cisplatin-resistant head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) patient-derived cell lines. We find that the epigenome can employ diverse modes to contribute towards cancer progression. Notably, we demonstrate that gene expression changes during cancer progression are co-modulated by alterations in both copy number and chromatin activity to drive epigenetic rewiring of cell-states. Furthermore, intratumor epigenetic heterogeneity may predispose sub-clonal populations within the primary tumor to acquire malignant characteristics. In conclusion, snCUT&RUN serves as a valuable addition to existing toolkit of single-cell assays to dissect the function of the epigenome during cancer progression.","gpl":"24676","gse":"212247","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/11/18","suppfile":"CSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6515815","title":"HN137Pri, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515809","title":"HN120Met, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515812","title":"HN120PCR, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515806","title":"HN120Pri, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515807","title":"HN120Pri, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515810","title":"HN120Met, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515819","title":"HN137Met, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515818","title":"HN137Met, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515821","title":"HN137PCR, biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6515822","title":"HN137PCR, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515816","title":"HN137Pri, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515813","title":"HN120PCR, biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6515817","title":"HN137Met, biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6515805","title":"HN120Pri, biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6515820","title":"HN137PCR, biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6515814","title":"HN137Pri, biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6515811","title":"HN120PCR, biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6515808","title":"HN120Met, biol rep1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":18,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39622638"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE212nnn/GSE212247/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA874706"},"200268740":{"uid":"200268740","accession":"GSE268740","gds":"","title":"Effects of AEBP1 knockdown on gene expression profiles in human skeletal muscle myoblasts","summary":"We recently identified adipocyte enhancer binding protein 1 (AEBP1) as a novel cancer stroma-associated gene and reported that AEBP1 activates cancer-associated fibroblasts in oral cancer. In this study, we show that AEBP1 is negatively associated with skeletal muscle cell differentiation. We found that AEBP1 is downregulated during the diffraction of human skeletal muscle myoblasts (HSMMs). Microarray analysis revealed that knockdown of AEBP1 significantly upregulated a number of genes associated with myogenesis in HSMMs. Notably, co-culture of HSMMs with oral squamous cell carcinoma cells resulted in downregulation of muscle-related genes and upregulation of AEBP1 in HSMMs.","gpl":"16699","gse":"268740","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/11/14","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8298245","title":"HSMM_d3"},{"accession":"GSM8298248","title":"HSMM_siCONT"},{"accession":"GSM8298249","title":"HSMM_siAEBP1-1"},{"accession":"GSM8298246","title":"HSMM_d8"},{"accession":"GSM8298250","title":"HSMM_siAEBP1-2"},{"accession":"GSM8298247","title":"HSMM_d8_SAS"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39521917"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE268nnn/GSE268740/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1118342"},"200235417":{"uid":"200235417","accession":"GSE235417","gds":"","title":"Evaluation of gene expression changes upon acquired resistance to anti-EGFR treatment in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"We deciphered molecular mechanisms associated with acquired resistance to anti-EGFR targeted therapy in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) by comparing gene expression profiles in cetuximab-sensitive and -resistant patient-derived xenograft (PDX) models of HNSCC. We generated and validated several HNSCC PDX models. Resistance mechanisms to anti-EGFR therapy were investigated in one of these PDX models (UCLHN04). First, sensitivity to cetuximab treatment was tested. This model showed high sensitivity to this drug. We induced acquired resistance to anti-EGFR therapy in this sensitive model by treating it chronically with anti-EGFR monoclonal antibody (cetuximab, 30 mg/kg/week) until resistance ensues. RNA-seq analysis was performed on samples coming from untreated and cetuximab-resistant PDX, revealing major changes of expression at the mRNA level.","gpl":"24676","gse":"235417","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/11/08","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7502137","title":"PDX, baseline UCLHN04 biol rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7502135","title":"PDX, baseline UCLHN04 biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7502132","title":"PDX, resistant to cetuximab UCLHN04 biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7502133","title":"PDX, resistant to cetuximab UCLHN04 biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7502136","title":"PDX, baseline UCLHN04 biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7502134","title":"PDX, resistant to cetuximab UCLHN04 biol rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE235nnn/GSE235417