 identify novel radiosensitizers. Up-regulation of the SNHG6 micropeptide is associated with radioresistance of HNSCC cells. To assess the mechanism SNHG6-mediated radioresistance, we performed transcriptome analyses of RTR61 cells expressing either siScramble, or siSNHG6.","gpl":"24676","gse":"217642","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/11/01","suppfile":"RESULTS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6723871","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siSNHG6, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6723868","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siScramble,rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6723874","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siScramble, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6723870","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siSNHG6, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6723867","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siScramble,rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6723876","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siSNHG6, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6723873","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siScramble, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM6723875","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siScramble, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6723878","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siSNHG6, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6723872","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siSNHG6, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM6723877","title":"RTR61 cells, cytoplasmic RNA, siSNHG6, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM6723869","title":"RTR61 cells, total RNA, siScramble,rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE217nnn/GSE217642/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA899876"},"200252265":{"uid":"200252265","accession":"GSE252265","gds":"","title":"Multiparameter imaging reveals clinically relevant cancer cell – stroma interaction dynamics in head and neck cancer","summary":"Epithelial tumors are characterized by abundant inter- and intra-tumor heterogeneity, which complicates diagnostics and treatment. The contribution of cancer-stroma interactions to this heterogeneity is poorly understood. Here we report a paradigm to quantify phenotypic diversity in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) with single cell resolution. By combining cell state markers with morphological features, we identify phenotypic signatures that correlate with clinical features, including metastasis and recurrence. Integration of tumor and stromal signatures reveals that partial epithelial-mesenchymal transition (pEMT) renders disease outcome highly sensitive to stromal composition, generating a strong prognostic and predictive signature. Spatial transcriptomics and subsequent analyses of cancer spheroid dynamics identifies cancer-associated fibroblast–pEMT axis as a nexus for intercompartment signaling that reprograms pEMT cells into an invasive phenotype. Taken together, we establish a paradigm to identify clinically relevant tumor phenotypes, and discover a cell state-dependent interplay between stromal and epithelial compartments that drives cancer aggression.","gpl":"24676","gse":"252265","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Other","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/10/28","suppfile":"CSV, H5, JSON, MTX, PNG, TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7998253","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH12"},{"accession":"GSM7998259","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH24"},{"accession":"GSM7998256","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH20"},{"accession":"GSM7998255","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH19"},{"accession":"GSM7998258","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH22"},{"accession":"GSM7998252","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH8"},{"accession":"GSM7998254","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH17"},{"accession":"GSM7998257","title":"HNSCC-KP_Batch1_UH21"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39471809"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE252nnn/GSE252265/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1059032"},"200251902":{"uid":"200251902","accession":"GSE251902","gds":"","title":"Multiparameter imaging reveals clinically relevant cancer cell – stroma interaction dynamics in head and neck cancer","summary":"Epithelial tumors are characterized by abundant inter- and intra-tumor heterogeneity, which complicates diagnostics and treatment. The contribution of cancer-stroma interactions to this heterogeneity is poorly understood. Here we report a paradigm to quantify phenotypic diversity in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) with single cell resolution. By combining cell state markers with morphological features, we identify phenotypic signatures that correlate with clinical features, including metastasis and recurrence. Integration of tumor and stromal signatures reveals that partial epithelial-mesenchymal transition (pEMT) renders disease outcome highly sensitive to stromal composition, generating a strong prognostic and predictive signature. Spatial transcriptomics and subsequent analyses of cancer spheroid dynamics identifies cancer-associated fibroblast–pEMT axis as a nexus for intercompartment signaling that reprograms pEMT cells into an invasive phenotype. Taken together, we establish a paradigm to identify clinically relevant tumor phenotypes, and discover a cell state-dependent interplay between stromal and epithelial compartments that drives cancer aggression.","gpl":"18573","gse":"251902","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/10/28","suppfile":"H5AD, MTX, TSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7989695","title":"UTSCC76a"},{"accession":"GSM7989694","title":"UTSCC74a + CAF"},{"accession":"GSM7989693","title":"UTSCC74a"},{"accession":"GSM7989696","title":"UTSCC76a + CAF"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39471809"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE251nnn/GSE251902/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1055901"},"200274788":{"uid":"200274788","accession":"GSE274788","gds":"","title":"X-ray-induced more serious damage to CD8+ T cells in patients with hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (HPSCC) than those in healthy individuals","summary":"Radiotherapy (RT) is a commonly used clinical management for hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (HPSCC), which represents the most unfavorable prognosis among all subtypes of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma. However, radiation may cause lymphopenia, a significantly adverse event with detrimental prognostic implications for patients. While CD8+ T cells are vital in tumor immunity, the specific effects of RT on CD8+ T cells as well as the underlying mechanisms have not been clearly elucidated. Here we found that subpopulations of peripheral T lymphocytes exhibited differential profiles in patients with HPSCC compared to healthy individuals both pre- and post-irradiation. Importantly, CD8+ T cells from HPSCC patients showed greater reduction of cytokine production, more severe proliferation defect, and increased apoptosis compared to those of healthy individuals after in vitro irradiation. Mechanistically, ATM-Chk2 pathway mediated the attenuated radiation-induced apoptosis of CD8+ T lymphocytes from HPSCC patients. Our study demonstrated that CD8+ T cells in patients with HPSCC are more sensitive to the radiation-induced damage than those in healthy individuals. Therefore, our findings present a potential immunotherapeutic target for safeguarding CD8+ T cells against radiation-induced damage.","gpl":"24676","gse":"274788","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/10/26","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8457931","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8457928","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, untreated, 24h, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8457925","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8457929","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8457926","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, untreated, 24h, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8457920","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, untreated, 24h, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8457923","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8457921","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, untreated, 24h, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8457927","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, untreated, 24h, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8457924","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8457922","title":"CD8+ T cells from healthy individuals, untreated, 24h, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8457930","title":"CD8+ T cells from patients with HPSCC, irradiated (2Gy), 24h, rep2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE274nnn/GSE274788/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1148058"},"200278692":{"uid":"200278692","accession":"GSE278692","gds":"","title":"IL-12 drives the expression of the inhibitory receptor NKG2A on human tumor-reactive CD8 T cells","summary":"Blockade of NKG2A/HLA-E interaction is a promising strategy to unleash the anti-tumor response. Yet the regulation of NKG2A expression on CD8+ lymphocytes and the T cell anti-tumor response are still poorly understood. Here, by performing CITE-seq on human T cells derived from head and neck squamous cell carcinoma and colorectal cancer, we show that NKG2A expression is induced in tumor-infiltrating  CD8+ T cells differentiating into cytotoxic, CD39+CD103+ double positive (DP) T cells. This developmental trajectory lead to TCR repertoire overlap between the NKG2A– and NKG2A+ DP CD8 T cells, suggesting shared antigen specificities. Mechanistically, IL-12 is essential for the expression of the NKG2A on naïve CD8+ T cells in a CD40/CD40L- dependent manner, in conjunction with TCR stimulation and increased TGF-β levels. Our study thus reveals that NKG2A is induced by IL-12 on human tumor-reactive CD8+ T cells exposed to a TGF-b-rich environment, highlighting an underappreciated immuno-regulatory feedback loop dependent on IL-12 stimulation.","gpl":"24676","gse":"278692","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Other","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/10/08","suppfile":"CSV, H5AD, RDS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8553037","title":"HNSCC P4 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553034","title":"HNSCC P2 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553040","title":"HNSCC P5 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553028","title":"CRC P4 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553031","title":"HNSCC P1 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553032","title":"HNSCC P1 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553035","title":"HNSCC P3 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553029","title":"CRC P5 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553026","title":"CRC P3 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553023","title":"CRC P2 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553027","title":"CRC P4 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553024","title":"CRC P2 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553030","title":"CRC P5 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553021","title":"CRC P1 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553042","title":"HNSCC P6 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553036","title":"HNSCC P3 TIL ADT"},{"accession":"GSM8553041","title":"HNSCC P6 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553033","title":"HNSCC P2 TIL GEX"},{"accession":"GSM8553039","title":"HNSCC P5 TIL GEX"