","title":"Noncanonical Formation of SNX5 Gene-Derived Circular RNA Regulates Cancer Growth","summary":"To investgate the funcitonal role of STAU1 on the formation of circSNX5. We performed gene expression profiling analysis using data obtained from RNA-seq of 2 different cells (replicate)","gpl":"18573","gse":"213634","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/06/19","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6590580","title":"Ctrl cells, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM6590583","title":"shSTAU1 cells, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM6590581","title":"Ctrl cells, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM6590582","title":"shSTAU1 cells, replicate 1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["39155279"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE213nnn/GSE213634/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA881998"},"200205380":{"uid":"200205380","accession":"GSE205380","gds":"","title":"YTHDC1 promotes stemness maintenance and malignant progression in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"RNA sequencing was used to detect differential expressed genes (DEGs) between SCC9-sphere and SCC9 cell line and to uncover molecular pathways and target molecules associated with cancer stem cells.","gpl":"16791","gse":"205380","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/06/14","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6211175","title":"h1076si1"},{"accession":"GSM6211176","title":"h1076si2"},{"accession":"GSM6211174","title":"h1076C"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":3,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE205nnn/GSE205380/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA844915"},"200262574":{"uid":"200262574","accession":"GSE262574","gds":"","title":"Germline-specific RNA helicase DDX4 forms cytoplasmic granules in cancer cells and promotes tumor growth","summary":"Cancer cells undergo major epigenetic alterations and transcriptomic changes, including ectopic expression of many tissue- and cell type-specific genes. Here we show that the germline-specific RNA helicase DDX4 (DEAD-box helicase 4) forms germ granule-like cytoplasmic ribonucleoprotein (RNP) granules in various human tumors, but not in cultured cancer cells. These cancerous DDX4-complexes contain RNA-binding proteins and splicing regulators, including many known germ granule components. The deletion of DDX4 in cancer cells induces transcriptomic changes and affects the alternative splicing landscape of a large number of genes involved in cancer growth and invasiveness, leading to compromised capability of DDX4-null cancer cells to form xenograft tumors in immunocompromised mice. Importantly, occurrence of DDX4-granules is associated with poor survival in patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, and higher histological grade of prostate cancer. Taken together, these results show that the germ granule-resembling cancerous DDX4-granules control gene expression and promote malignant and invasive properties of cancer cells.","gpl":"16791","gse":"262574","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/06/13","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8171879","title":"PC3_tumor, DDX4_NULL, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM8171882","title":"PC3_tumor, DDX4_NULL, replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM8171885","title":"PC3_cells, control, replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM8171888","title":"PC3_cells, DDX4_NULL, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM8171877","title":"PC3_tumor, control, replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM8171886","title":"PC3_cells, control, replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM8171880","title":"PC3_tumor, DDX4_NULL, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM8171883","title":"PC3_cells, control, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM8171889","title":"PC3_cells, DDX4_NULL, replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM8171884","title":"PC3_cells, control, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM8171887","title":"PC3_cells, DDX4_NULL, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM8171875","title":"PC3_tumor, control, replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM8171878","title":"PC3_tumor, control, replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM8171876","title":"PC3_tumor, control, replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM8171881","title":"PC3_tumor, DDX4_NULL, replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM8171890","title":"PC3_cells, DDX4_NULL, replicate 4"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":16,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38963760"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE262nnn/GSE262574/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1003658"},"200226620":{"uid":"200226620","accession":"GSE226620","gds":"","title":"CD161 expression of TRM cells counteracts the HPV-associated clinical benefit in oropharyngeal cancer immunotherapy","summary":"Oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (OPSCC), a distinct head and neck cancer subtype that develops in the oropharynx, is well known to be categorized into human papillomavirus induced (HPV+) and non-HPV induced (HPV-). While HPV+ OPSCC is reported to be clinically advantageous compared to HPV- OPSCC, the heterogeneous responses in HPV+ OPSCC during immunotherapy treatment have not been well characterized at the molecular level. In this study, we assess both tumor and immune cells of the OPSCC tumor microenvironment (TME) via single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and single-cell TCR sequencing (scTCR-seq). By dissecting the transcriptome of OPSCC tumor cells, we find that cancer cell transcriptional diversity may be a strong factor in negating HPV associated clinical benefits. By assessing immune cells, we observed molecular antiviral and anti-tumor characteristics of T cells that is associated with HPV infection, and key cell-cell interaction differences among resident memory T cells (Trm), follicular helper T cells, and B cells. Importantly, we identify a novel molecular state within the HPV+ OPSCC that is distinguished by the expression KLRB1 (CD161) in the Trm that inhibits anti-tumor activity, which may further explain the heterogeneous clinical benefits of HPV+OPSCC. Association of KLRB1 expression of Trm and immunotherapy outcome was confirmed via immunofluorescence analysis, suggesting CD161 as a novel therapeutic target to improve cancer treatment of HPV+ OPSCC.","gpl":"20795","gse":"226620","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Other","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/06/01","suppfile":"MTX, RDS, TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7080444","title":"P31, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080441","title":"P25, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080430","title":"HPV04, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7157427","title":"P20, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7157433","title":"P30, negative, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080427","title":"HPV01, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7157430","title":"P27, negative, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080433","title":"HPV07, negative"},{"accession":"GSM7080439","title":"P20, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080436","title":"HPV10, negative"},{"accession":"GSM7157425","title":"P18, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7157428","title":"P23, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080428","title":"HPV02, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080445","title":"P30, negative"},{"accession":"GSM7080442","title":"P27, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7157434","title":"P32, negative, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7157431","title":"P28, negative, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080431","title":"HPV05, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080443","title":"P28, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080434","title":"HPV08, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080429","title":"HPV03, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080446","title":"P32, negative"},{"accession":"GSM7157426","title":"P29, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7157429","title":"P25, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080440","title":"P23, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080437","title":"P18, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080438","title":"P29, positive"},{"accession":"GSM7080435","title":"HPV09, negative"},{"accession":"GSM7157432","title":"P31, positive, TCR"},{"accession":"GSM7080432","title":"HPV06, negative"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":30,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38857913"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE226nnn/GSE226620/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA940826"},"200262334":{"uid":"200262334","accession":"GSE262334","gds":"","title":"Effect of ZNF582 on gene expression in human head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) is highly aggressive and associated with poor prognosis. The molecular mechanisms underlying HNSCC progression are important for the development of novel diagnostics and interventions. The bio-functional effects and potential mechanisms of ZNF582 are still unknown. We established HNSCC cell lines with ZNF582 overexpressed. High-throughput RNA-sequencing was performed to identify the altered signaling pathways in HNSCC with ZNF582 overexpression.","gpl":"24676","gse":"262334","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/05/31","suppfile":"XLS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8163415","title":"Zp"},{"accession":"GSM8163414","title":"Np"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":2,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE262nnn/GSE262334/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1091350"},"200268537":{"uid":"200268537","accession":"GSE268537","gds":"","title":"Investigating a Novel Interaction of c-MET and BACH1 in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma Metastasis","summary":"Metastasis is estimated to be responsible for 90% of cancer deaths, and fewer than 10% of patients with metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) survive beyond 5 years.  HNSCC is responsible for upwards of 270,000 global deaths annually, with up to 30% more cases projected annually by 2030.  The proto-oncogene MET encodes for the tyrosine kinase receptor c-MET, which is overexpressed in over 80% of human papilloma virus (HPV)-negative HNSCC cases and particularly enriched in metastatic lymph nodes.  c-MET is activated by its ligand, hepatocellular growth factor (HGF), and is known to promote cancer cell migration, proliferation, and metastasis through a variety of downstream effectors.  Thus far, unfortunately, inhibition of c-MET has shown low efficacy as a single-agent therapy in clinical trials, which indicates a need for further understanding of the mechanisms underlying c-MET-mediated metastasis in HNSCC.  We show here that human HNSCC cells upregulate expression of the transcription factor BACH1 through c-MET activation upon HGF treatment.  In accordance with previous reports, HGF activation increased expression of epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) markers.  Similarly, BACH1 suppression reduced expression of these EMT markers.  By pharmacological inhibition of c-MET by FDA-approved capmatinib in combination with hemin treatment to reduce BACH1 expression, migration was reduced compared to either treatment alone in scratch-wound migration assays.  Collectively, these data indicate that BACH1 and c-MET are both necessary for the regulation of EMT and migration in HNSCC.  Our data suggest the potential for combination therapy targeting both c-MET and BACH1 to reduce HNSCC metastasis.","gpl":"24676","gse":"268537","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/05/30","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8294013","title":"Detroit562 shBACh1 cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294010","title":"Detroit562 shControl cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294007","title":"Cal27 shBACh1 cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294019","title":"FaDu shBACh1 cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294005","title":"Cal27 shBACH1 cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294016","title":"FaDu shControl cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294002","title":"Cal27 shControl cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294008","title":"Detroit562 shControl cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294014","title":"FaDu shControl cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294011","title":"Detroit562 shBACH1 cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294003","title":"Cal27 shControl cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294017","title":"FaDu shBACH1 cells, untreated"},{"accession":"GSM8294006","title":"Cal27 shBACH1 cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294009","title":"Detroit562shControl cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294015","title":"FaDu shControl cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294012","title":"Detroit562 shBACH1 cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294018","title":"FaDu shBACH1 cells, 3 hours HGF treatment"},{"accession":"GSM8294004","title":"Cal27 shControl cells, 6 hours HGF treatment"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":18,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE268nnn/GSE268537/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1117756"},"200220863":{"uid":"200220863","accession":"GSE220863","gds":"","title":"Histological Pattern of Tumor Inflammation and Stromal Density Correlate with Patient’s Demographics and Immuno-Oncologic Transcriptional Profile in Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma","summary":"The aim of this work is to demonstrate the correlation of Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma (OSCC) tumor inflammation and stromal density with patient demographics and with the differential gene expression (DGE) analysis of 8 OSCC tumor tissue. Using the IO-360 nanoString platform, we showed that at a transcriptional level, decreased cytokine signaling and activation of oncogenic pathways were observed in immune excluded tumors and absence of peritumoral stromal inflammation. Smokers, and males correlated with decreased global levels of immune cells and predominance of myeloid populations.","gpl":"27956","gse":"220863","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/05/19","suppfile":"RCC","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6824720","title":"Patient 3 OSCC4"},{"accession":"GSM6824719","title":"Patient 2 OSCC3"},{"accession":"GSM6824722","title":"Patient 5 OSCC6"},{"accession":"GSM6824725","title":"Patient 9 OSCC10"},{"accession":"GSM6824718","title":"Patient 1 OSCC1"},{"accession":"GSM6824721","title":"Patient 4 OSCC5"},{"accession":"GSM6824724","title":"Patient 8 OSCC09"},{"accession":"GSM6824723","title":"Patient 7 OSCC08"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38903181"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE220nnn/GSE220863/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA911764"},"200235223":{"uid":"200235223","accession":"GSE235223","gds":"","title":"Transcriptional characterization of adjuvantly radio(chemo)therapy treated head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC).","summary":"We characterized n=134 HNSCC by Quantseq 3´-RNA-sequencing for their tran