M7497320","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_129"},{"accession":"GSM7497237","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_017"},{"accession":"GSM7497257","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_043"},{"accession":"GSM7497340","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_151"},{"accession":"GSM7497297","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_103"},{"accession":"GSM7497271","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_054"},{"accession":"GSM7497294","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_100"},{"accession":"GSM7497323","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_132"},{"accession":"GSM7497326","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_136"},{"accession":"GSM7497217","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_013"},{"accession":"GSM7497300","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_106"},{"accession":"GSM7497303","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_112"},{"accession":"GSM7497220","title":"HNSCC_LMU_KKG_Sample_025"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":134,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38783045"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE235nnn/GSE235223/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA985582"},"200252287":{"uid":"200252287","accession":"GSE252287","gds":"","title":"MUC1-C PROMOTES CHRONIC INFLAMMATION IN THE PROGRESSION OF HEAD AND NECK SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMAS","summary":"Mucin 1 (MUC1) is aberrantly expressed in adenocarcinomas of epithelial barrier tissues and contributes to their progression. Less is known about involvement of MUC1 in the pathogenesis of squamous cell carcinomas (SCCs). The present work demonstrates that the MUC1 gene is upregulated in advanced head and neck SCCs (HNSCCs) and, as determined by scRNA-seq analysis, is expressed in the malignant cell populations of HNSCC tumors. Studies of HNSCC cell lines demonstrate that the MUC1-C subunit regulates expression of (i) RIG-I and MDA5 pattern recognition receptors, (ii) STAT1 and interferon (IFN) regulatory factors, and (iii) downstream IFN-stimulated genes (ISGs). MUC1-C integrates STAT1 with induction of the ∆Np63 and SOX2 genes that are aberrantly expressed in HNSCCs. We also show that MUC1-C/STAT1 signaling activates the NFE2L2 gene and, in turn, MUC1-C binds directly to NRF2 in regulating redox balance. In extending these HNSCC cell dependencies, we demonstrate that MUC1-C is necessary for NOTCH3 expression, self-renewal capacity and tumorigenicity. These results indicate that MUC1-C is a common effector that integrates STAT1-mediated chronic inflammation with regulation of the ∆Np63, SOX2, NRF2 and NOTCH3 TFs. Our findings indicate that MUC1-C drives progression of the HNSCC CSC state and is a target for advanced HNSCC treatment.","gpl":"18573","gse":"252287","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/04/23","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7998643","title":"CAL27_DOX_NEG_R1"},{"accession":"GSM7998652","title":"HSC3_DOX_PLUS_R1"},{"accession":"GSM7998649","title":"HSC3_DOX_NEG_R1"},{"accession":"GSM7998648","title":"CAL27_DOX_PLUS_R3"},{"accession":"GSM7998646","title":"CAL27_DOX_PLUS_R1"},{"accession":"GSM7998651","title":"HSC3_DOX_NEG_R3"},{"accession":"GSM7998645","title":"CAL27_DOX_NEG_R3"},{"accession":"GSM7998654","title":"HSC3_DOX_PLUS_R3"},{"accession":"GSM7998650","title":"HSC3_DOX_NEG_R2"},{"accession":"GSM7998653","title":"HSC3_DOX_PLUS_R2"},{"accession":"GSM7998647","title":"CAL27_DOX_PLUS_R2"},{"accession":"GSM7998644","title":"CAL27_DOX_NEG_R2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38619287"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE252nnn/GSE252287/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1059261"},"200256352":{"uid":"200256352","accession":"GSE256352","gds":"","title":"Arginine methylation-mediated FXR1 binding with RNAs in cancer cells","summary":"FXR1 is an RNA binding protein and it is post traslationally modified by a methyltrasferase to facilitate its RNA binding activity FXR1 is methylated by PRMT5, an arginine methyltransferase, to promote its stability and facilitate its association with mRNAs. Both genetic and small molecule PRMT5 inhibition failed to methylate recombinant as well as the endogenous FXR1, resulting in protein instability and downregulation of FXR1 target mRNA levels in HNSCC cells. To study the differentially regulated gene under the loss of PRMT5, we knocked- down the PRMT5 using shRNA and confirmed the transcript and protein levels using qRT PCR and immunoblot respectively.","gpl":"24676","gse":"256352","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/04/08","suppfile":"TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8094181","title":"UMSCC74B cells, PRMT5 KD-1"},{"accession":"GSM8094180","title":"UMSCC74B cells, Control-2"},{"accession":"GSM8094182","title":"UMSCC74B cells, PRMT5 KD-2"},{"accession":"GSM8094179","title":"UMSCC74B cells, Control-1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38709899"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE256nnn/GSE256352/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1079254"},"200253091":{"uid":"200253091","accession":"GSE253091","gds":"","title":"The Small Extracellular Vesicle-Mediated Intercellular Transformation of CXCR1Low to CXCR1High Tumor Cells Promotes the Progression of Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma","summary":"The heterogeneity of tumor cells enables cancers to dynamically adapt to microenvironmental stresses during progression. However, the mechanism underlying the transformation and intercellular communication between heterogeneous tumor cells has remained elusive. Here, we report a “contagion model” that mediates intercellular transformation between heterogeneous tumor cells which facilitates tumor progression. Initially identifying heterogeneous expression of CXCR1, a receptor for interleukin-8, in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) tumor cells. Following interleukin-8-mediated activation, CXCR1High cells transformed CXCR1Low cells into CXCR1High cells through the secretion of small extracellular vesicles (sEVs), which increased the proportion of CXCR1High cells and facilitated tumor progression. Mechanistically, we demonstrate that sEVs derived from interleukin-8-activated CXCR1High cells contain high levels of ATP citrate lyase (ACLY), which acetylates NF-κB p65 and facilitates its nuclear translocation to transcribe CXCR1 in CXCR1Low cells. That process could be inhibited by Bempedoic acid, an FDA-approved ACLY-targeted drug. Taken together, our study reveals an sEV-mediated transformation of CXCR1Low to CXCR1High cells that promotes HNSCC progression. This provides a new paradigm to explain the dynamic changes of heterogeneous tumor cells, and identifies Bempedoic acid as a potential drug for HNSCC treatment.","gpl":"24676","gse":"253091","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/04/03","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8013601","title":"HN6 cells, without treatment-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013627","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with vector plasmids-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013624","title":"HN6 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013604","title":"HN6 cells treated with IL-8-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013621","title":"HN6 cells transfected with vector plasmids-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013630","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013610","title":"Cal27 cells treated with IL-8-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013607","title":"Cal27 cells, without treatment-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013613","title":"HN4 cells transfected with vector plasmids-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013629","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013626","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with vector plasmids-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013609","title":"Cal27 cells, without treatment-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013606","title":"HN6 cells treated with IL-8-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013618","title":"HN4 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013598","title":"HN4 cells treated with IL-8-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013595","title":"HN4 cells, without treatment-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013611","title":"Cal27 cells treated with IL-8-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013617","title":"HN4 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013614","title":"HN4 cells transfected with vector plasmids-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013615","title":"HN4 cells transfected with vector plasmids-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013620","title":"HN6 cells transfected with vector plasmids-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013612","title":"Cal27 cells treated with IL-8-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013600","title":"HN4 cells treated with IL-8-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013597","title":"HN4 cells, without treatment-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013623","title":"HN6 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013603","title":"HN6 cells, without treatment-3"},{"accession":"GSM8013619","title":"HN6 cells transfected with vector plasmids-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013616","title":"HN4 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013602","title":"HN6 cells, without treatment-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013608","title":"Cal27 cells, without treatment-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013605","title":"HN6 cells treated with IL-8-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013599","title":"HN4 cells treated with IL-8-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013596","title":"HN4 cells, without treatment-2"},{"accession":"GSM8013622","title":"HN6 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013628","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with plasmids contained ACLY-1"},{"accession":"GSM8013625","title":"Cal27 cells transfected with vector plasmids-1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":36,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38545803"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE253nnn/GSE253091/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1064027"},"200255878":{"uid":"200255878","accession":"GSE255878","gds":"","title":"FER kinase governs invasive tumor growth through dynamic control of oncogenic growth factor receptor activity","summary":"EGFR antagonism using monoclonal antibodies remains the only approved and targeted first line treatment option for non-resectable head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), yielding a modest 13% response rate. While it is clear that alternative growth factor receptor (GFR) signals govern refractory behavior towards EGFR antagonism, it remains unknown which upstream mechanisms control subcellular GFR activation. Here, we show that FER, a non-receptor tyrosine kinase, controls invasive tumor growth through dynamic GFR activation and recycling. Using patient derived organoid (PDO) models HNSCC we demonstrate that FER is essential for invasive growth in collagen extracellular matrix (ECM) networks. We show that inactivation of FER impairs growth factor dependent phosphorylation of EGFR-Y1068 and MET-Y1234/5. Additionally, FER controls ligand dependent endocytic recycling, and endocytic velocity, implying multifactorial functional regulation of proximal receptors during HNSCC invasion. Loss of function experiments using either genetic intervention or a FER PROteolysis-TArgeting Chimera (PROTAC) strategy in PDO-based xenograft mouse models, demonstrate that FER is essential for invasive growth and metastasis of HNSCC. Finally, we demonstrate direct clinical relevance by showing that FER expression levels correlate with poor survival in HNSCC patients. In sum, we conclude that FER is a master regulator of HNSCC invasive growth and metastasis, and nominate FER as a prominent candidate for targeted clinical intervention in HNSCC patients.","gpl":"18573","gse":"255878","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/03/31","suppfile":"MTX, TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8082162","title":"T4_Dox_Neg_Replicate2_scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8082161","title":"T4_Dox_Neg_Replicate1_scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8082164","title":"T4_Dox_Pos_Replicate2_scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8082163","title":"T4_Dox_Pos_Replicate1_scRNAseq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["41115375"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE255nnn/GSE255878/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1076893"},"200247024":{"uid":"200247024","accession":"GSE247024","gds":"","title":"m1A inhibition fuels oncolytic virus-elicited antitumor immunity via downregulating PD-L1 expression","summary":"RNA N1-methyladenosine methylation (m1A) modification is critical in regulating mRNA translation and thus protein synthesis, but the role of m1A modification in the occurrence, progression, and immunotherapy of head and neck squamous cell cancer (HNSCC) remains largely unknown. In Tgfbr1/Pten 2cKO mice, we found that the spontaneous neoplastic transformation of oral mucosa is accompanied by elevated levels of m1A modification. Analysis of m1A-associated genes identified TRMT61A as the key m1A writer associated with cancer progression, and poor prognosis. Mechanically, TRMT61A-induced tRNA-m1A modification promotes MYC protein synthesis and subsequent programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) expression. In Tgfbr1/Pten 2cKO mice, RNA-m1A modification levels are also elevated in tumors that developed resistance to oncolytic herpes simplex virus (oHSV) treatment. Therapeutic inhibition of m1A modification sustains oncolytic virus-induced antitumor immunity and reduces tumor growth, providing a promising strategy for alleviating resistance to oHSV therapy. These findings indicate that m1A inhibition can prevent immune escape after oHSV therapy by reducing the expression of PD-L1. Our results provide a mutually reinforcing strategy for clinical combination immunotherapy.","gpl":"29480","gse":"247024","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/03/31","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7882262","title":"WSU-HN6, sh61A, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882256","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, IFNγ, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882259","title":"WSU-HN6, shCtrl, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882253","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, IFNγ, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882247","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, PBS, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882250","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, PBS, rep1"},{"accession":"GSM7882254","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, IFNγ, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882251","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, PBS, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882248","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, PBS, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882249","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, PBS, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7882263","title":"WSU-HN6, sh61A, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882257","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, IFNγ, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882260","title":"WSU-HN6, shCtrl, rep2"},{"accession":"GSM7882264","title":"WSU-HN6, sh61A, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7882261","title":"WSU-HN6, shCtrl, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7882252","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, PBS, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7882255","title":"CAL-27, shCtrl, IFNγ, rep3"},{"accession":"GSM7882258","title":"CAL-27, sh61A, IFNγ, rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":18,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["38730256"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE247nnn/GSE247024/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1035770"},"200262483":{"uid":"200262483","accession":"GSE262483","gds":"","title":"DNA methylation status determines the sensitivity of repeats to restriction by the HUSH-MORC2 complex [ONT cDNA sequencing]","summary":"The human silencing hub (HUSH) complex binds to transcripts of LINE-1 retrotransposons (L1s) and other genomic repeats, recruiting MORC2 and other effectors to remodel chromatin. However, how HUSH and MORC2 operate alongside DNA methylation, a central epigenetic regulator of repeat transcription, remains poorly understood. Here we interrogate this relationship in human neural progenitor cells (hNPCs), a somatic model of brain development that tolerates removal of DNA methyltransferase DNMT1. Upon loss of MORC2 or HUSH subunit TASOR in hNPCs, L1s remain silenced by robust promoter methylation. However, genome demethylation and activation of evolutionarily-young L1s attracts MORC2 binding. Simultaneous depletion of DNMT1 and MORC2 causes massive accumulation of L1 transcripts. We identify the same mechanistic hierarchy at pericentromeric α-satellites and clustered protocadherin genes, repetitive elements important for chromosome structure and neurodevelopment respectively. Our data delineate the independent epigenetic control of repeats in somatic cells, with implications for understanding the vital functions of HUSH-MORC2 in hypomethylated contexts throughout human development.","gpl":"26167","gse":"262483","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2024/03/26","suppfile":"BW","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8170413","title":"ONT_cDNAseq_LV3367_DNMT1_CRISPRcut"},{"accession":"GSM8170410","title":"ONT_cDNAseq_LV3599_Control"},{"accession":"GSM8170411","title":"ONT_cDNAseq_LV3688_MORC2_CRISPRi"},{"accession":"GSM8170412","title":"ONT_cDNAseq_LV3691_TASOR_CRISPRi"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa